A||||6||Denise||Brakes||||1||
Z||000000||Denise||04-30-2000||06:30 PM||||Hi!<BR>I noticed the Fiero Store is selling a front brake upgrade kit. about $300.00  not bad I guess. I"m wondering if it is the same as the Beretta upgrade?  Yes I know I could ask the Fiero store too.  LOL   <BR>Lessee: Beretta rotors, calipers, s-10 MC, go here go there... get that, pick up this...   Maybe just call the Fiero store...||||reg||1
Z||000001||GREENDANO||04-30-2000||07:13 PM||||You need to do the math.  $50 to get a set of hubs machined, $25 for wheel bearings, $30/pair rotors, $50/both calipers with pads.  Almost half the money.  The Blazer MC isn't needed, I didn't put it on my F40 after installing it and then taking it off because of the pedal feel that I had, It was unsafe to say the least.  <BR>  Just my $0.02  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  Dan-O||||reg||7
Z||000002||batboy||04-30-2000||08:41 PM||||I've mentioned in past posts about planning on using a S-10 master cylinder, but have been told recently that the S-10 one won't work. I don't know for sure though. You may have to use a M/C designed for 4 wheel disc brakes. Some of the guys are using full size blazer M/C's. I'm still waiting to see how that works out.<P>Not that I don't believe Dan-o, but us scientists like to have lots of data.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||sluppy123||04-30-2000||10:45 PM||||I replaced the master cylinder on one of my 87' GT's with a new one from a Full size Blazer along with the vented rotors from the Beretta. There is a big diff. between the pedal travel and feel in the GT I did the swap on and the GT left stock. The pedal travel is a lot less, but more pressure is required, But they grab alot sooner than the stock brakes. ||||reg||10
Z||000004||triker||05-01-2000||03:08 PM||||I replaced the back breaks on my 3 wheeled Fiero Saturday morning with the Bereta front breaks.  It took about 3 hours and $175. Now I can lock them up when before I couldn't.  I was kind of dissapointed that the Bereta's were cast iron instead of aluminum like the Fiero's.  About 8 lbs heavier for the pair.  I was able to get about 5 lbs back by throwing out the emergancy break and all the associated cables.  Who needs an emergancy break on a trike anyway?  I had a little more break pedal travel, but not enough to hurt.  I wouldn't make the swap on just the back of my 87 GT.  I'd think it would get kind of scary during hard breaking.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Dan||05-01-2000||06:00 PM|||| I did the rear brake upgrade on my 86 SE and turned the stock rotors and new pads on the front.My front brakes will lock up first,but I believe it is because the system is metered for the size of caliper piston on the Fiero caliper.The Baretta caliper is larger than the fiero caliper.I think I would have to be doing a lot of braking to get the fronts hot enough to fade to get the rear to lock-up first.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan||05-01-2000||06:03 PM|||| sorry, I meant to say the caliper piston is larger,(requires more fluid) thus the difference in pedal and front locking up first.||||reg||1
